rationale:
The test was whether a 10-16 year old who joins alone, or with one friend, gets a goal, a payoff and a reason to return within the first five minutes. CAREER is the only proposal whose core loop fully works for one player: a fictional artist's request at 1:00, live green ticks while building, DELIVER with stars and an NPC reaction at 3:00, and Cred spent on rack gear at 4:00. After that come daily requests and a streak tomorrow, a Weekly Single and plaques next week, and a new artist and rank for every level, which finally fills levels 5-10. It pays for making music, not for pressing buttons. It gives players visible status (rank nameplate, a personal credits list that mirrors OK's real wall). It adds content cheaply (one Data/Artists.lua entry per artist) and breaks none of the hard constraints.

SOCIAL has the best drama and the best engineering order (MonitorService queue first). But its Cypher needs 2 or more players, its solo fallback is a bare star grade, and with 2 players 'one pick, not your own' always ties. Most early servers will look like the empty voting servers the research warned about. CREATIVE has the best instant start (presets, genre at level 1) and the cleverest 2-player mechanic (SAMPLE IT / flips). But it front-loads the format v3 rewrite (about 850 lines and every DAW test) before any new gameplay is visible, its melody depends on partner samples the research did not find, and a sandbox alone gives no goal to chase.

The plan is CAREER as the spine with the strongest pieces from the other two grafted in:
- from SOCIAL: the monitor queue moved into Milestone A, the multiplayer test hooks, neon pulsing on BeatBus, and positive-only FIRE;
- from CREATIVE: starter presets, the genre basics in the first session, and the XP rebalance;
- in B: flips as the first social layer;
- later: a 3+ player Cypher and Friday event, and an async Weekly Flip once the user answers the music-library question.

All three proposals build the same rule-checker, so it ships once and powers requests, dailies and later battles. The code confirms the key must-fixes: the untouched-default save pays XP and completes the tour (CellsDiffer(nil, p) returns 80), airings are 4 fixed one-bar loops (about 6.8 s), the monitors refuse when busy with no queue, the genre features are locked to levels 2-4, mic spam is the best XP strategy, and explicit titles are still on by default.
